course: dictionary
lesson: 5731
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. A lory is a real bird: a small, brightly coloured parrot from Australia, New Guinea and nearby islands. «The pool has become crowded with a Duck and a Dodo, a Lory and an Eaglet» is a list of creatures, so the bird meaning fits.
explain: In Alice in Wonderland, Lory is also the name of a bird character. «The Lory says "Ugh!", then pretends it did not speak» shows a character with feelings and words, not just a parrot on a branch. Ask yourself: does the sentence fit the real bird, or a character who talks? The clues tell you which one.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Alice... argues with the Lory, who only says "I am older than you, and must know better"» only makes sense if the Lory is a character who can speak and argue. Your meaning must always fit every clue in the sentence.
fact: Lory means a small, brightly coloured parrot from Australia, New Guinea and nearby islands.
fact: Lories and lorikeets are real birds, and lorikeets are their smaller cousins.
fact: A lory has a brush-tipped tongue for eating nectar and pollen from flowers.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the Lory is a bird character who argues with Alice.
fact: The Lory says «I am older than you, and must know better», and refuses to tell its age.
exercise: «The pool has become crowded with a Duck and a Dodo, a Lory and an Eaglet.» What is a Lory here? | A big bird that died out long ago | A young eagle | *A small, brightly coloured parrot | A pool of tears
exercise: Lorikeets are the smaller cousins of lories. What is a lorikeet? | *A smaller relative of the lory | A large eagle | A type of flower | A character who argues in Alice
exercise: Which clue shows that the Lory in Alice is a talking character, not a real parrot? | It has a brush-tipped tongue | *It says «I am older than you, and must know better» | It lives in Australia | It is brightly coloured
test: What does a lory use its brush-tipped tongue for? | *Eating nectar and pollen from flowers | Climbing trees | Swimming in the pool | Singing to Alice
test: Why will the Lory not tell Alice its age? | It is asleep | It is too busy eating | *It refuses to say how old it is | It cannot count past two
exam: What can the word lory mean? | Only a fish from the sea | Only a country in Australia | *Both a real parrot and a bird character in Alice | Only a type of tree
